    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0433" n="343" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg343"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.31/49</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Hornbeck</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-12-31">December 31, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Doctor Hornbeck</hi>: I brought Admiral Reeves’
                        statement<anchor n="52" xml:id="d251-f1"/> to the attention of the President
                    this morning and expressed regret that the announcement had come on the very day
                    of the denunciation of the Treaty by Japan.<anchor n="53" xml:id="d251-f2"/> The
                    President also expressed his regret that there had been any publicity
                    whatsoever; he said, however, that the maneuvers in the Pacific must take place;
                    he thought it might be a good plan to let Mr. Grew know that the maneuvers were
                    purely of a defensive character; that last year they were in the neighborhood of
                    Hawaii and its tributary islands, this year they would be merely extended <pb
                        facs="0434" n="344" xml:id="pg344"/> north, but not to a point west of the
                    Aleutian Islands. I told the President that I had come to him with the
                    suggestion that the maneuvers be moved to the other side of the Canal or
                    confined closer to our coast and that I thought this would be a fine gesture to
                    make. It was evident, however, that the President had given his approval to the
                    maneuvers as proposed and there was nothing further to be done about it.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d251-f1" n="52" xml:id="d251-f1-content">
                <p>Admiral Joseph M. Reeves, commander in chief of the United States Fleet,
                    announced on December 29 at San Pedro, Calif., that the North Pacific Ocean
                    would be a theater of naval maneuvers from May 3 to June 10, 1935.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d251-f2" n="53" xml:id="d251-f2-content">
                <p>See note from the Japanese Ambassador, <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_274"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 274</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
